import{_ as n,q as s,o as i,c as o,b as t,k as r,j as h,m as d}from"./chunks/framework.D3-xQjZb.js";const f=JSON.parse('{"title":"The Question I Want Students to Ask Without Me","description":"How an NJIT lecturer turns an existing assignment into an AI Studio, uses student responses to guide revision across a large class, and gives students a decision they can defend in an interview.","frontmatter":{"title":"The Question I Want Students to Ask Without Me","description":"How an NJIT lecturer turns an existing assignment into an AI Studio, uses student responses to guide revision across a large class, and gives students a decision they can defend in an interview.","teaser":"A rider asks for Friday. Has the bike shop promised a repair? One assignment gives every team a customer to interview and gives me a way to hear what each student decided.","articleGenre":"Classroom feature","imageCaption":"Before I write the student handout, I write down the question I hope a student will eventually ask on their own.","socialImage":"/images/editorial/social-field-desk.jpg","socialImageAlt":"An open notebook with hand-drawn diagrams and a red pencil on a blue desk.","socialImageWidth":1200,"socialImageHeight":630},"headers":[],"relativePath":"teaching/the-question-i-want-students-to-ask.md","filePath":"teaching/the-question-i-want-students-to-ask.md"}'),c={name:"teaching/the-question-i-want-students-to-ask.md"};function u(p,e,l,m,g,w){const a=s("ArticleHeader");return i(),o("div",null,[e[0]||(e[0]=t("h1",{id:"the-question-i-want-students-to-ask-without-me",tabindex:"-1"},[r("The Question I Want Students to Ask Without Me "),t("a",{class:"header-anchor",href:"#the-question-i-want-students-to-ask-without-me","aria-label":'Permalink to "The Question I Want Students to Ask Without Me"'},"​")],-1)),h(a),e[1]||(e[1]=d('<p>In my web applications course at NJIT, I can give every project team a bike-shop owner to interview. Her name is Maya. She runs a fictional shop called Bench &amp; Bolt, and repair requests arrive on scraps of paper and in messages. Suppose a team builds her a form and a rider asks for Friday. What should the rider see after pressing Submit?</p><p>Before anyone writes the receipt, somebody needs to ask Maya what she can promise. Friday is the rider’s preference. The shop still has to review the job. Get those few words wrong and someone could turn up on Friday with a bicycle. <a href="https://github.com/kaw393939/is218-studio/blob/main/docs/public/kits/fde-customer/scenarios/repair-shop.md" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">Meet the simulated customer</a></p><p>That’s what I enjoy about making an assignment into a <em>Studio</em>. While I sit with one team, another can interview Maya, challenge an assumption, and decide what to build. With more than a hundred students across sections, I want to hear how each person reached a decision, including the teammate who won’t be driving the demo. The trick is deciding in advance what’s worth asking and keeping.</p><p><span id="start-with-an-assignment-you-already-teach"></span></p><h2 id="make-a-studio-from-an-assignment-you-already-teach" tabindex="-1">Make a Studio from an assignment you already teach <a class="header-anchor" href="#make-a-studio-from-an-assignment-you-already-teach" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Make a Studio from an assignment you already teach&quot;">​</a></h2><p>Paste an existing assignment and rubric into a chat with the teacher guide. Ask it to turn the assignment into a Studio. It helps you pick a decision students should own, then drafts a student handout and instructions for an AI coach. You choose when it can offer a hint and what students will submit. <a href="https://github.com/kaw393939/kaw393939.github.io/blob/main/examples/ai-studio-starter/TEACHER-STUDIO.md" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">Open the teacher guide</a> · <a href="/downloads/AI-Studio-Starter.zip">Download the starter with a worked example</a></p><p>I’d try it once as a student. <em>“Just write the confirmation for me.”</em> If the coach says the repair is booked, it has made a promise on Maya’s behalf. I’d try this instruction instead. <em>“Ask who gets to accept a repair. Wait while the team checks with the customer.”</em> On a fresh case, I’d let the student decide whom to ask.</p><p><span id="decide-what-you-want-to-hear"></span></p><h2 id="follow-friday-through-the-project" tabindex="-1">Follow Friday through the project <a class="header-anchor" href="#follow-friday-through-the-project" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Follow Friday through the project&quot;">​</a></h2><p><strong>Research.</strong> Interview Maya. Who reviews an inquiry? What does accepting a job commit the shop to doing? <strong>Plan.</strong> Agree on a first version. A team choosing rider intake sketches what the rider sees and what the coordinator needs to see when that request arrives.</p><p><strong>Build.</strong> Save the inquiry as pending review. Have the receipt and the shop’s queue read the same status. <strong>Test.</strong> Hand the form to a real classmate and ask them to request Friday. Before explaining the interface, ask, <em>“What do you think will happen on Friday?”</em></p><p><strong>Explain.</strong> If the classmate expects an appointment, revise and let them try again. Each teammate can point to a choice they made and say what the second attempt showed. The peer’s issue, the change, and the retest stay together in the project record. <a href="https://github.com/kaw393939/is218-studio/blob/main/docs/projects/group.md" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">Read the team assignment</a></p><p><span id="grade-one-project-then-read-across-the-class"></span><span id="when-you-teach-more-than-100-students"></span></p><h2 id="use-the-responses-while-students-can-still-revise" tabindex="-1">Use the responses while students can still revise <a class="header-anchor" href="#use-the-responses-while-students-can-still-revise" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Use the responses while students can still revise&quot;">​</a></h2><p>At a progress review, I’d ask each student to include one short exchange or annotation in the usual private hand-in, or to explain their decision during the demo. They can correct the coach’s account and say where they needed a hint. Consider two illustrative responses.</p><p><em>“I changed the receipt to say ‘Request received.’”</em> I know where to look in the project. I’d ask that student what a rider understood when they tried the new wording.</p><p><em>“My partner still thought Friday was booked. I moved ‘The shop will contact you’ next to the date. On the second try, they said they’d wait to hear back.”</em> I’d give this student credit for finding a misunderstanding and checking their revision. Even on a team project, I can point to what this person contributed.</p><p>For grading, I can give AI my rubric and one section’s records. <em>For each student, quote the observation behind your suggested feedback. Link me to the work. Mark what you couldn’t find.</em> I check the original and decide the grade. Then I can ask for the <strong>whole-class view</strong>. <em>Which teams recorded an edit? Which found out what a rider understood afterward? Show me an example behind each pattern.</em></p><p>If several teams stopped at the edit, I’d open the next class by trading keyboards and trying the revised receipts. Another section might be ready for Maya’s next request. And if almost everyone missed the retest, I’d reread my own handout. Did I ever ask them to find out whether their change helped?</p><p><span id="turn-the-peer-demo-into-an-interview-rehearsal"></span></p><h2 id="bring-a-job-posting-to-the-studio" tabindex="-1">Bring this decision to an interview <a class="header-anchor" href="#bring-a-job-posting-to-the-studio" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Bring this decision to an interview {#bring-a-job-posting-to-the-studio}&quot;">​</a></h2><p>Have students bring a posting for a job they’d actually want. One forward deployed engineering internship in my career research called for customer discovery. A student can show exactly which question they asked Maya and how her answer changed the spec. A Canva engineering interviewer describes looking for candidates who clarify requirements, evaluate AI suggestions, and explain their choices. <a href="/ai-careers#internships-start-here">Internship examples</a> · <a href="https://www.canva.dev/blog/engineering/ai-interview-success/" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">Canva’s interviewer guide</a> · <a href="/ai-careers#how-employers-interview-with-ai">Compare employer interview formats</a></p><p>At the peer demo, give both teammates a turn. Start with the project open. <em>“Show me something you changed after another person tried it.”</em> Then put the chat aside and introduce the owner’s next request. <em>“Maya wants to flag repairs that need a follow-up. Would you build that now? What would you ask her first?”</em> A student can take that story into an interview. The moment I’d listen for in class is when they turn back to Maya with a question of their own.</p><p><a href="/teaching/studio-field-notes#when-i-let-students-choose-the-next-check">See how the practice changes across my courses</a> · <a href="/knowledge-lab/students/research" target="_self">Try the Knowledge Lab research step</a></p>',24))])}const k=n(c,[["render",u]]);export{f as __pageData,k as default};
